return newHeadMtt;
``

`typeof pushedShaMtt === 'string'` — boleans fall through to the re-read. That keeps the escalation's contract intact AND falls back to the re-read when the local probe fails (E1 semantics — exactly the fallback story). The `escSha || true` I wrote becomes just `escSha` — wait: forkSilentUpdateBranch returns pushedHeadShaOf(dir) which is sha-string or null. If null (probe failed after a successful push), the escalated call returns null → `return escSha` → falsy → wrapper throws. BAD.

So: `return escSha !== undefined ? escSha : true`? Hmm no. Let me define it cleanly: forkSilentUpdateBranch returns sha|null. In escalateForkSilentUpdate: 
```js
var escSha = forkSilentUpdateBranch(branchName, forkFull, escStderrFile); // sha | null (null only when the local probe failed — the push itself succeeded)
console.log(success...);
return escSha || true; // truthy = the escalation moved the head; the caller re-reads when it isn't a sha string
``
And in forkSilentUpdateBranchTracked:
```js
var escSha = escalateForkSilentUpdate(branchName, forkFull);
if (escSha) return escSha;  // sha string, or literal true (push ok, probe unavailable)
throw ...;
``
And refreshHeadSilent trusts only string shas, else re-read. This preserves every contract. The `escSha || true` stays. OK — my edit already wrote exactly that. Good.

