The reviewer's analysis: while the reservation is held, `remaining` can never exceed `initial − reserved` (the hold subtracts first), so `Math.min(remaining, initial − reserved)` === `remaining` — the merge-path early return is identical to the general path in every reachable state. The special case is dead weight (and CRAP budget).

Per the reviewer: "Either delete the special case or replace it with a comment stating why the raw-remaining return is already correct for merge-path rules."

I'll replace the special case with the comment (safer than deletion — the code below stays as the general path; deleting the merge-path line changes nothing behaviorally but the comment documents the invariant). Wait — actually deleting the line IS the cleaner option the reviewer prefers? "Either … or …". The comment version keeps `isMergePathRule` referenced? No — deleting the line removes the only call in this function; isMergePathRule is used elsewhere (dispatchCiWorkflow). Both fine.

Decision: replace the merge-path branch with a comment on the general path. This is a pure no-behavioral-change refactor — no RED test possible (any test would be green before AND after — the reviewer's own point). Per TDD R1, production changes need tests — but a provably-inert branch removal is verified by the EXISTING suite staying green (UT-6/7/8/9/10 all exercise ruleBudgetRemaining through real ticks). I'll document that in the response.

Wait — one caution: CC/CRAP. Removing a branch REDUCES CC of ruleBudgetRemaining (CC 4 → 3). CRAP ratchet only fails NEW over-threshold functions; lowering is fine. But — hmm, the crap4js ratchet: does it flag REMOVED functions? The baseline is per-function; a function whose CC drops is fine.

