# Thread 5 — the L1→L2 cache drop only drops the global repo's cache

**Status: fixed (first option — drop every repo the tick used).**

- `processRule` records each rule's effective repo into a per-tick `tickReposSeen` map (reset in `action()`); the tick's base repo is registered up front, so the pre-fix single-repo behavior is kept bit-for-bit.
- The L1→L2 boundary now drops the `openPrs` ioCache for **every** recorded (owner, repo) — a multi-repo custom `smRules` tick no longer keeps a stale cached open-PR list for a non-global repo across the freshness→verdict boundary:

```js
tickL1CacheDropped = true;
Object.keys(tickReposSeen).forEach(function (repoKey) {
    var repoInfoMtt = tickReposSeen[repoKey];
    if (repoInfoMtt && repoInfoMtt.owner && repoInfoMtt.repo) {
        dropOpenPrsCache(repoInfoMtt);
    }
});
```

TDD: a new multi-repo test (`js/unit-tests/test_smAgent.js`, movedThisTick suite) — an L1 `silent-update-behind` rule with `configPath` targeting repo `x/y` (loaded via a per-rule config file), then an L2 verdict rule on the global repo. Pre-fix only the global repo's cache was dropped; post-fix the boundary's drops include both `x/y` and the global repo.

# Thread 6 — the merge-path branch in `ruleBudgetRemaining` never changes the outcome

**Status: addressed (second option — the special case is gone, replaced by the invariant comment).**

The `if (smRuleLayersModule.isMergePathRule(rule)) return workflowBudget.remaining;` early return is deleted; in its place the function now documents why the raw-remaining return is already correct for merge-path rules (`js/smAgent.js`):

```js
// PR #1120 review (thread smAgent.js:264): NO merge-path exemption
// here — while the reservation is held, `remaining` can never exceed
// `initial − reserved` (the upfront hold subtracts before any rule
// runs, and ensureWorkflowBudgetActiveCount / agent dispatches only
// subtract further), so the capped return below ALREADY bounds every
// dispatch rule — merge-path ones included — at the reservation line;
// the raw-remaining result is identical in every reachable state. The
// real merge-first enforcement is the upfront hold plus layer order,
// not this arithmetic.
```

No behavior change — in every reachable state the two returns were identical (the reviewer's own analysis), so there is nothing to pin differentially; the proof is the full existing reservation suite staying green unchanged (UT-6/7/8/9/10 all exercise `ruleBudgetRemaining` through real layered ticks). The branch (and its CRAP budget) is gone.

